// THE TURBA PHILOSOPHORUM IN PLAIN WORDS: a close paraphrase of each unit of
// A. E. Waite's English (London, 1896) of the Latin Turba: the Epistle of
// Arisleus, the seventy-two Dicta and the Conclusion. Each entry names the
// sage who speaks the Dictum ("Iximidrus says ..."), as Waite prints the name
// before 'saith', and keeps the other voices apart: the assembly answers as
// "the Turba", and other sages who interject (Pythagoras, Arisleus, Parmenides,
// Bonites and others) are named where they speak. The names are the text's
// attributions, not historical authorship: nothing here says that Pythagoras,
// Plato or Socrates said these things. The alchemical terms the text leaves
// unexplained (Ethelia, Kuhul, Cambar, Corsufle, Ixir and the like) are kept
// as they are, with a gloss only where the text itself gives one or the
// meaning is plain (crocus, saffron colour; Rubigo, rust). Waite's footnotes
// were not imported and are not reported, save one of his bracketed
// conjectures, which is said to be his.

export const PLAIN_TURBA: Record<string, string> = {
  "arab.turba.epistle":
    "Arisleus writes, as a follower of Pythagoras and a disciple of his disciples, to all who come after, wishing them health and mercy. He says that his master Pythagoras the Italian, master of the wise and chief of the prophets, had a greater gift of God and of wisdom than anyone since Hermes. So Pythagoras decided to gather his disciples, who had grown many and had become leading figures everywhere, to discuss this most precious Art, so that what they said would be a foundation for those who came later. He told Iximidrus, a man of the highest counsel, to speak first.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1–9: the elements and the world
  "arab.turba.1":
    "Iximidrus says that the beginning of all things is a certain everlasting Nature, equal to all things, and that the visible natures, which are born and decay, are the times in which we see where that Nature leads them. The stars are fiery, and the air holds them in check: if the moist, dense air did not stand between the sun's flames and living things, the sun would burn up every creature, so God made the air to separate them. The air wins because it takes the sun's heat into its own heat and the water's moisture into its own moisture; the sun's heat draws fine vapour up from the water, and that moisture feeds the air, which in turn holds back the fire. Fire (hot and dry) and water (cold and moist) are enemies with no kinship; the air, warm and moist, sits between them and makes peace. From the fine vapour of the air, heat joined with moisture, a wind arises, which becomes spirit and life to all creatures. All this is arranged by God's will, and lightning flashes when the sun's heat strikes and breaks a cloud. The Turba (the assembly) replies that he has described fire well, as he knows it, and has trusted his brother's word.",
  "arab.turba.2":
    "Exumdrus says that he too praises the air, after Iximidrus's great speech, because it improves the work. Air thickens and thins, warms and cools: it thickens when the sun is far off in the heavens, and thins and warms when the sun is high. It is like spring, neither hot nor cold, and winter changes as the seasons change; when the sun moves away from the air it thickens and people feel the cold. The Turba says that he has described the air excellently.",
  "arab.turba.3":
    "Anaxagoras says that the beginning of everything God created is weight and proportion; weight rules all things, and weight is found only in bodies. The heaviness of all four elements comes to rest in earth: the heaviness of fire falls into air, air's (with fire's) into water, and water's (with that of fire and air) into earth, so earth is the densest of all. The Turba agrees and asks which element is the finest. He answers: fire, being hot and dry; air is less fine, being hot and moist; water less fine again, being cold and moist; and earth least, being cold and dry. Pythagoras says that the Sons of the Doctrine have described well the four natures from which God made all things, and that whoever understands this will find no greater purpose in the world. He asks them to go on; they ask him to call on each in turn, and Pandolfus is called.",
  "arab.turba.4":
    "Pandolfus says that air is a fine part of water and is never separated from it: air hidden in the water under the earth holds the earth up, so that it neither sinks into the water nor is flooded by it. The air's task is to fill gaps and keep apart things that differ, water and earth, and to make peace between enemies, water and fire, so that they do not destroy each other. The Turba asks for an illustration. He gives the egg, in which four things are joined: the shell is earth, the white is water, the thin skin under the shell is the air that divides earth from water, the yolk is fire, and the skin round the yolk is the other air that divides water from fire; both are one air, the lower thicker and the upper finer, being nearer the fire. At the centre of the yolk, apart from the four, is 'the point of the Sun', which is the chick. That is why all the philosophers of this art have used the egg as their example and set it over their work.",
  "arab.turba.5":
    "Arisleus says that the earth is a hill, not a plain: that is why the sun does not rise over every zone of the earth in the same hour, as it would if the earth were flat. Parmenides says that he has spoken briefly. Arisleus answers that the Master left them nothing that says otherwise; and he declares that God is one, never begotten and never begetting, and that after God the chief of all things are earth and fire, because fire is fine and light and rules everything on earth, and earth, heavy and coarse, holds up everything that fire rules.",
  "arab.turba.6":
    "Lucas says that they all talk only of the four natures, each noticing something about them, and declares that everything God created comes from these four and goes back into them; in them living things are born and die, as God has ordained. Democritus, Lucas's pupil, says that Lucas has spoken well. Arisleus tells Democritus that, as his knowledge comes from Lucas, it is presumptuous of him to speak among people who know his master. Lucas answers that though Democritus learned natural science from him, that knowledge came from the philosophers of India and from the Babylonians, and he thinks Democritus surpasses his own generation in it. The Turba replies that when he is older he will be well worth hearing, but while young he should keep quiet.",
  "arab.turba.7":
    "Locusta says that the creatures Lucas described are really only two kinds, and one of them cannot be known or spoken of except through devotion, because it is neither seen nor felt. Pythagoras asks him to say what this unseen, unfelt, unknown thing is. Locusta says it is known in this world by reason, without the senses that serve reason: only sight tells white from black, hearing a good word from a bad, smell a sweet scent from a foul one, taste sweet from bitter, and touch smooth from rough. When the Turba presses him, he says the thing known in none of these five ways is a sublime creature, perceived by reason alone, and Nature admits that God shares in that reason. He explains further: this world has a light, the sun, finer than every other nature, so that living things can see, and the moon, stars and fire all take their light from it. The sublime creature does not need the sun's light, because the sun is beneath it; its own light, brighter than the sun's, is taken from God's still finer light. The world is made of two dense things and two fine ones, but there is nothing dense in the sublime creature. The Turba praises him and asks him to say something more to revive their hearts, which folly has deadened.",
  "arab.turba.8":
    "Pythagoras says that God existed before all things and nothing was with him. When God was alone he made four things, fire, air, water and earth, and out of them all others, high and low. The angels were made from one element, fire; the sun, moon and stars from two, fire and air, so the angels, of one finer substance, shine more brightly; heaven was made of water and air. From three elements came beasts (fire, air and earth), flying things (fire, air and water) and plants (earth, water and air). When the Turba suggests that plants contain fire too, he agrees: it comes from the fine fire hidden in the air, but elemental fire is found only in things that have spirit and soul. Adam and his sons were made of all four. Whatever God made from one essence does not die until the Day of Judgement, for death is the coming apart of what is compounded, the separation of the soul from the body, and what is simple cannot come apart. Nothing compounded eats, drinks or sleeps without fire, for fire is what eats; the angels do not eat because it is thick fire that eats, and they are made of the thinnest, simplest fire. Arisleus asks for definitions of the four elements; Pythagoras says he will place that teaching where the foolish will not recognise it and the Sons of the Doctrine will not miss it, for it is the key, the perfection and the end.",
  "arab.turba.9":
    "Eximenus says that God made all things by his word, saying 'Be', and they were, along with the four elements, earth, water, air and fire, which he brought together; though they are hostile to one another, God united them in peace so that they love each other. From them come heaven and its throne, the angels, the sun, moon and stars, the earth and the sea and all their various creatures, which differ because they are made of different elements mixed together: mixed, the dry and moist, the hot and cold, lose their own natures. Creatures that come from the mixture never reach perfection unless they are left to putrefy and visibly decay. Of the four elements two, earth and water, can be seen and touched; the other two cannot, and their power is known only through earth and water. Unmixed, the four achieve nothing; mixed, they leave their own natures and become something else. Then he turns to the Art: no true tincture is made except from 'our copper', so they should not waste their brains and money. The basic rule is to turn the copper white and make visible 'coins', then turn it red until a tincture results. Burn the copper, break it up, take away its blackness by cooking, soaking and washing until it is white, and then govern it.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 10 onwards: the Art
  "arab.turba.10":
    "Arisleus says that the key of the work is the art of Coins. Take the body he has shown and beat it into thin plates; put them in the Water of our Sea, which is Permanent Water, cover them and set them over a gentle fire until they melt into waters, or Etheliae (the same thing). Mix, cook and simmer until a broth ('Brodium') forms, like 'Saginatum'. Stir in its water of Etheliae until it thickens and the coins turn many-coloured: this is called the Flower of Salt. Cook it until its blackness goes and whiteness appears; then grind it, mix it with the Gum of Gold, and cook until it becomes red Etheliae. Be patient in pounding. Soak the Ethelia in its own water, which came from it and is also Permanent Water, until it turns red: this is Burnt Copper, the leaven and flower of gold. Cook it with Permanent Water until all the water is used up and it becomes a very fine powder.",
  "arab.turba.11":
    "Parmenides says that envious writers have written at length about many waters, broths, stones and metals to mislead seekers. Leave all that, and make the white red from 'our copper', with copper and lead for the blackness and tin for melting. Unless they match like with like they will achieve nothing, because natures meet their own natures and rejoice; in them they putrefy and are born again, since Nature is ruled by Nature, which destroys it and renews it again and again. They should learn what corrupts and renews this nature, how love and enmity arise among natures, and how to reconcile them in the fire. The Wise put the whole work in one saying: 'Nature rejoices in Nature, and Nature contains Nature.' So take quicksilver, fix it in the body of Magnesia, in Kuhul, or in sulphur that does not burn; whiten it and put it on our copper when that is white; cook further and it becomes red, and further still gold. Gold is reddened only by Permanent Water: soak it seven times, cooking and washing, until it is red. He praises this heavenly, spiritual nature. When it tinges, it overcomes what is mixed with it, inside as well as on the surface; joined, the volatile and the fixed both withstand the fire. One matter overcomes four, and 'our Sulphur' alone consumes all things. The Turba says he has spoken excellently but has not shown how the smoke is to be handled and whitened.",
  "arab.turba.12":
    "Lucas says that he will follow the ancients, and that this is not the beginning of the procedure. Take quicksilver 'from the male' and fix it; laid on iron, tin or governed copper it will whiten. White Magnesia is made the same way, and because the magnet and iron are akin, 'our nature rejoices'. Cook the vapour with its own body until tin is produced, wash away its blackness and roast it at an even fire until white; every body is whitened by governed quicksilver, for Nature converts Nature. Take Magnesia and the waters of alum, nitre, the sea and iron, and whiten with smoke, which whitens everything because it is white itself; roast this white copper until it sprouts of itself. Whiten the white sulphurous nature with salt and dew, or with the Flower of White Salt, which is 'Ethel from Ethelia', and boil it seven days until it is like gleaming marble. Whiten Mardek with Gadenbe (wine and vinegar) and Permanent Water, roast and fix it; keep the fire gentle, for if it blazes the matter reddens too soon, and at the start you need the white; afterwards fix it until it turns red. Once fixed, it is called the Soul. One thing makes the work, though many operations go into it, and it changes nature at every stage. The Turba says that if he speaks as briefly as the Wise, those who do not want to stay in darkness will follow him.",
  "arab.turba.13":
    "Pythagoras says that there is another procedure, from the same root, differing only in name. The natures the envious describe as agreeing are one in taste, though they look as different as possible. The thing they describe in so many ways follows and reaches its companion without fire, as the magnet follows iron, and is compared to a seed and a womb. Embracing its companion, it brings out many colours, because this one thing is found everywhere: 'a stone, and also not a stone; common and precious; hidden and concealed, yet known by everyone; of one name and of many names', which is the Spume of the Moon. It is not a stone because it is more precious; without it Nature does nothing; its many names honour its excellence. Asked for some, he gives White Ethelia, White Copper, and 'that which flies from the fire and alone whitens copper'. Break the White Stone, fix it with milk, grind the calx without letting the moisture escape, fix it in the vessel until it is ash, and cook it with Spume of the Moon. Mix the gum with the ash and the dregs, moisten with permanent water, and roast more strongly if it has not become powder; the more the colours change, the more you should heat it. White quicksilver or Spume of the Moon, broken with a gentle fire, sets into a stone from which many colours appear. If anything is unclear, do as told until a white, flashing stone is produced.",
  "arab.turba.14":
    "Acsubofen thanks Pythagoras for speaking without envy, and Pythagoras wishes him freedom from envy too. Acsubofen says that sulphurs are contained in sulphurs, and moisture in moisture. The moisture is a venom: entering a body, it dyes it a colour that never changes and will not let the soul part from the body. That is what the envious mean when they say one flees and the other pursues until one seizes the other, for Nature has caught its equal. So from the mixture of sulphurs comes a precious colour that neither changes nor flees the fire, when the soul enters the body and dyes it. He then gives the same teaching as a recipe for Tyrian (purple) dye: treat the creature called Kenckel, whose water is all of Tyrian colour, with gentle fire until it becomes earth; add back its moisture little by little, cook with sea water until dry, and keep soaking, cooking and drying until it has drunk it all, then leave it until the purple rises. Prepare it with boys' urine, sea water and permanent water, cook until the blackness goes and then until it turns red; keep soaking and cooking, washing with sea water, and a colour will appear such as the Tyrians never made. To deepen it, dye alternately with gum soaked in permanent water and dry in the sun. Purple is dyed only by cold: steep wool in cold water until it draws out the dye's strength, which the philosophers call the Flower, and leave it until it takes the most precious Tyrian colour.",
  "arab.turba.15":
    "Frictes says that the foundation of this Art, for which many have perished, is one thing only: stronger than all natures and most sublime to the philosophers, though fools think it the commonest thing of all. If kings knew it, no philosopher would ever obtain it. How this nature turns body into spirit and overcomes everything! Asked by Pythagoras to name it, Frictes says it is a very sharp vinegar, which turns gold into pure spirit; without it neither whiteness, blackness, redness nor rust can be made. Mixed with the body it becomes one with it, turns it into spirit and dyes it with a colour that cannot be removed; on the fire without it the body burns and spoils. The first moisture is cold, so beware of the fire: govern gently until the sulphur cannot burn. A little of this sulphur burns a strong body, which is why the Wise name it at the beginning of their books. The vinegar burns the body to ash and whitens it, and cleared of blackness it becomes a stone, a coin of intense whiteness; cook the stone until it breaks down, then dissolve and temper it with sea water. The work begins with whitening, then reddening, then perfecting. He swears that he searched books for a long time and prayed God to teach him what this one thing is; God showed him clean water, by which he knew pure vinegar, and the more he read, the more he understood.",
  "arab.turba.16":
    "Socrates says that no tincture can be made without Lead, and recalls how thrice-great Hermes infused the red into the body so that its colour no longer changed. The first power is vinegar, the second the Lead of the Wise, which makes bodies unchangeable and dyes them fast. Take Lead made from the stone called Kuhul and cook it until black; grind it with Water of Nitre until it is like grease, cook it in a bright fire and pour off the water, until the stone is clean and very white. Grind it with dew and sun, sea water and rain water for 21 days until it is like a metallic stone; cook it with water of nitre until it melts into tin, and again until dry; do not burn it, for this is what they call incombustible sulphur. Cook it with the sharpest vinegar for 150 days. That is the white lead; everything after is 'women's work and child's play'. The secret of making gold comes from the male, in the lead, and the female, in orpiment. Mix them, for the female rejoices to receive the male's strength and gives him a dyeing spirit; grind them in a glass vessel with Ethelia and sharp vinegar, cook for seven days without letting the secret smoke away, and if it dries, soak it again in vinegar. Orpiment is the woman by whom the greatest secret is accomplished. Do not show these things to the wicked. By the Ethelia of vinegar God perfects the work, and bodies become spiritual.",
  "arab.turba.17":
    "Zimon says that they have spoken of whitening, but reddening remains. Unless you whiten you cannot redden, because the two natures are simply red and white: whiten the red and redden the white. The year has four seasons: winter, of a cold make-up; spring, of the make-up of air; summer, of fire; and autumn, when fruit ripens. Govern your natures the same way: dissolve in winter, cook in spring, fix in summer, and gather and dye the fruit in autumn; if you go wrong, blame no one but yourselves. The Turba asks for another teaching, and he speaks of making lead red: take the copper the Master told you to take at the beginning of his book, combine lead with it, cook until thick, then set and dry it until red. This is the Red Lead the wise spoke of: copper and lead become a precious stone. Mix them equally and roast gold with them, and if well governed it becomes a dyeing spirit among spirits. When the male and female are joined, the result is not a volatile wife but a spiritual compound, and from the compound turned into a red spirit comes 'the beginning of the world'. This Red Lead belongs to their work, and nothing is done without it.",
  "arab.turba.18":
    "Mundus tells the Turba that the philosophers have described gum in many ways, but it is nothing but permanent water, from which their precious stone is born; many seek it and few find it. The gum is improved only by gold. Many experimenters find things that waste away in the work, but what is made from the gum and the honoured stone that has taken the tincture never diminishes. Their gum is held more honourable than gold or pearls by those who know it, yet without gold it cannot be improved. So the philosophers did not write the procedure plainly, lest fools learn it and sell it cheap. The recipe: one part each of the whitest gum, urine of a white calf, gall of a fish and 'body of gum'; mix and cook for forty days, then dry in the sun. Cook it in turn with milk of ferment, fig milk and the milk of grass root, drying each time; moisten with rain water and dew, cook until dry, soak with permanent water and dry thoroughly. Mix it with the gum that has every colour and cook strongly until the water's force and the body's moisture are gone; then leave it forty days, grinding or cooking, until the spirit enters the body. So the spirit becomes bodily and the body spirit. Watch that it does not fly off as fumes; then open the vessel and you will find what you wanted. This is the secret of gum that the philosophers hid in their books.",
  "arab.turba.19":
    "Dardaris says that the earlier Masters described Permanent Water, and a beginner should attempt nothing until he knows its power, for it must be used in every mixing, grinding and step of the work; without it nothing is achieved, and whoever does not understand it may not enter the Art. Its strength is a spiritual blood, which is why it is called Permanent Water: ground with the body, by God's will it turns the body into spirit. Mixed and made one, they transform each other: the body takes in the spirit, and the spirit takes the body into a dyed spirit, like blood. Whatever has spirit has blood as well. Remember this secret.",
  "arab.turba.20":
    "Belus says to the others, 'Disciples, you have spoken excellently.' Pythagoras asks why he calls philosophers disciples; Belus says it is to honour their Master, not to make them his equals; Pythagoras says that those who have composed this book, called the Turba, with him ought not to be called disciples. Belus goes on: they have often described Permanent Water and the making of the White and the Red, under many names, but in how they join weights, mixtures and procedures they agree with the hidden truth. Of this despised thing it is said that the Hidden Glory of the Philosophers is a stone and not a stone, called by many names so that fools will not recognise it. Some name it after the place where it forms, others after its colour, such as the Green Stone; others the Stone of the most intense Spirit of Brass, not to be mixed with bodies; others otherwise, because jewellers called 'saven' sell it for coins; some call it Spume of the Moon; some name it by astronomy or arithmetic. It has a thousand names, the best being 'That which is produced out of metals'; others call it the Heart of the Sun, or what is brought out of quicksilver with the milk of volatile things.",
  "arab.turba.21":
    "Pandolfus tells Belus that he has left nothing to add about the despised stone, but teaches that this stone is a permanent water, the water of worldly life, as in the philosophers' short saying 'Nature rejoices in Nature, Nature contains Nature, and Nature overcomes Nature'. No dyeing venom is made without the Sun and its shadow, and whoever dyes the venom of the wise from them has reached the highest secret. Their coin, when it turns red, is called gold, and whoever knows the philosophers' hidden Cambar knows the secret. The procedure: grind White Magnesia with wine, put it in its vessel, pray God, and cook it gradually until it is a black stone. Govern it toward the white until it becomes Kuhul, but do not let the blackness last more than forty days. Grind it with the flower of copper, gold of the Indies, and crocus, that is, fixed exalted alum, or the sign of Saturn; cook the four together for 40 or 42 days, and God will show you the beginning of the stone Atitos. Soak with the rest of the gum, drying and moistening the ash until it takes the colour you want. To perfect it, govern it with a third of the medicine, keeping two thirds for soaking it seven times until true earth has drunk them all, then put it on a hot fire until the earth draws out its flower. He repeats this, and by God's favour the stone of Tyrian colour will appear. He begs them by God not to reveal this secret to the wicked.",
  "arab.turba.22":
    "Theophilus praises Pandolfus; the Turba asks him to explain what Pandolfus said, without envy. He says that the secret of gold and the art of the coin are wrapped in a dark garment, and no one understands the philosophers' books without much reading, experiment and questioning of the Wise. Boritis and copper are akin, for the Wise men's boritis melts copper into a flowing water. Divide the venom into two equal parts: melt the copper with one and keep the other for grinding and soaking it; cook it in its own water for 42 days and you will find the copper turned to quicksilver. Wash it by cooking until it is like copper without a shadow, then cook it until it sets: this stone is called Boritis. Soak it repeatedly with the permanent water you kept until its colours show: this is the great putrefaction. The same kinship that exists between magnet and iron exists between copper and permanent water. Take white Magnesia and quicksilver, mix with the male and grind by cooking, not by hand, until the water is thin; cook in half the water for eleven, or else forty, days until a white flower appears like the flower of salt, and seal and cook forty days until it is water whiter than milk and clean of all blackness. Then wash it with the other half until a crocus (saffron colour) appears; the Iksir grinds itself; soak it until it is like pomegranate syrup, and cook until the colour the philosophers praise appears.",
  "arab.turba.23":
    "Cerus tells the Sons of the Doctrine that Theophilus has spoken of the kinship between magnet and iron while copper is properly governed for a hundred days, and asks what could be more useful than to say that there is no kinship between tin and quicksilver. The Turba says he has spoken badly and disparaged the true procedure. He insists he speaks only the truth and asks why they are angry; they tell him to say what he likes. He tells them to take quicksilver, which holds the male strength, and cook it with its body until it is a flowing water; cook the male with the vapour until both set into a stone. Of the water divided in two, one part is for melting and cooking the body, the other for cleansing what is burnt and its companion, the two being made one. Soak the stone seven times and cleanse it until it breaks down, is purged and becomes earth; in 42 days the whole turns to earth. Melt it by cooking until it is like true water, which is quicksilver; wash it with water of nitre until it is like a melted coin; cook until it sets like tin, which is a great secret: the stone made of two things. Govern it by cooking and grinding until it is an excellent crocus; water dried with its companion is what they call crocus. Cook it and soak it with the water you kept until you reach your goal.",
  "arab.turba.24":
    "Bocascus says to Belus that he has spoken well and he will follow him; the other tells him to do as he likes but not to be envious, which does not suit the Wise. Bocascus tells the Sons of the Doctrine to take lead and, as the philosophers ordered, soak, melt and set it until it is a stone; govern the stone with glue of gold and pomegranate syrup until it breaks down. With one of the two parts of the water they have already melted the lead into water; cook it until it is dry and has become earth, then grind it with the water kept back until it turns red. The Turba says he has only piled up ambiguous words and should return to the subject. He says that whoever wants to set quicksilver must mix it with its equal and cook it carefully until both become permanent water, then cook the water until it sets, drying it with its own equal vapour, for they have found the quicksilver set by itself. If they understand and put what is needed in the vessel, they should cook it until it sets and grind it until it is a crocus the colour of gold.",
  "arab.turba.25":
    "Menabdus blesses the previous speaker for his true and clear teaching; the Turba tells him not to fall short of the man he praises. Menabdus says he can add nothing, but advises making bodies into not-bodies, and the bodiless into bodies, for so the compound is prepared and its hidden nature drawn out. Join quicksilver and the body of Magnesia to these bodies, the woman with the man, and their secret Ethelia, which colours bodies, is drawn out. Quicksilver is a fire that burns, kills and breaks up bodies; the more it is ground with the body, the more the body breaks down and the quicksilver becomes fine and living. Ground and cooked properly, it gives Ethel, a fixed nature that takes every tincture and overcomes the fire; it does not colour until it is coloured, but once coloured it colours. No body can dye itself unless its spirit is drawn from its secret belly; a dense thing does not dye a fine one. When the body of copper has been governed and its finest part drawn out, that part becomes the tincture: as the wise man said, copper does not dye until it is dyed. The four bodies to be governed are this copper; the tinctures are the condensed, a joined vapour, and the moist, the water of sulphur, for sulphurs are held by sulphurs, and so Nature rejoices in Nature, overcomes and holds it.",
  "arab.turba.26":
    "Zenon says that the Wise have joined two bodies, which their Master never told them to do. The Turba asks for his own view, without envy. He says: let the compound putrefy for forty days, then sublime it five times in a vessel, put it on a dung fire and cook it, and these colours will appear: on the first day black citrine, on the second black-red, on the third like dry crocus, and finally purple. Then the ferment and the common coin are added, and the Ixir is made from the moist and the dry, and it dyes with a colour that does not change; this is called a body with gold in it. When making the Ixir, do not take it out in haste, for it is slow. This venom is like birth and life, because it is a soul drawn out of many things and laid on coins; its tincture is life to what it is joined with, taking away their faults, but death to the bodies it is drawn from. So the Masters said there is the same desire between them as between male and female, and whoever knows these natures will bear the tedium of cooking until he reaches his goal, by God's will.",
  "arab.turba.27":
    "Gregorius says that the envious have called the venerable stone Efflucidinus and told people to govern it until it shines like marble. Asked to explain, he says: mix the copper with vinegar and govern it until it becomes water; let it set, and it remains a stone shining like marble. Then govern it until it turns red, for cooked until it breaks down into earth it turns red; cook and soak it again and again until it has that colour and becomes hidden gold, and repeat until it is gold of Tyrian colour. When the stone shines, grind it to earth until it begins to redden, then soak it several times with the rest of the water the envious told you to divide in two, until colours hidden in no body appear; governed ignorantly, it shows none of them. He knew a man who did the work rightly but gave it up when the redness was slow to come, thinking he had made a mistake. So watch how you make the conjunction: the Punic dye, having embraced his bride, passes quickly into her body, melts, sets, breaks and dissolves it, and the redness soon follows; done without the right weight, death follows and the work is thought bad. Keep the fire gentle while melting, but strong once it has become earth, and soak it until God brings out the colours.",
  "arab.turba.28":
    "Custos says that he marvels at the great power of this water: entering the body, it turns it first into earth and then into powder. To test it, take it in the hand: if it is as impalpable as water it is excellent; if not, cook it again. Using any substance other than 'our copper' with 'our water' is useless; governing our copper with our water gives all they have promised. The Turba objects that the envious made things obscure by telling them to take lead and white quicksilver and govern them with dew and sun until a coin-like stone forms. Custos answers that the envious meant our copper and our permanent water, cooked on a gentle fire to give that coin-like stone, of which the Wise said that Nature rejoices in Nature because of the kinship between the two bodies, copper and permanent water: their nature is one, and without that kinship they would not unite so quickly into one. The Turba then asks why the envious tell them to take the copper they have made and roasted until it has become gold.",
  "arab.turba.29":
    "Diamedes (also called Diomedes in this Dictum) says, addressing Moses, that he has spoken generously, and he will confirm his words. A man comes only from a man, and animals and birds beget only their like: Nature is improved only by her own nature. So use venerable Nature, from which alone the Art comes. Join the male, son of the red slave, in marriage with his fragrant wife, and the Art is produced between them; add nothing foreign: that conception is enough, 'for it is near, yet the son is nearer still'. How precious is that red slave, without whom the procedure cannot last! Bacsen says that Diomedes has revealed this openly; he replies that he will make it plainer still, warns those who do not fear God that God may take the art from them, and asks why they are envious of their brothers; they say they hide only from fools. He goes on: put Citrine with his wife into the bath, not too hot, lest they lose sense and motion, until their body and colour become one; give back their sweat, let it die again and rest. Honour the king and his wife and do not burn them. Cook them until they turn black, then white, then red, and finally until a dyeing venom is produced. Those who do not understand should blame themselves, not the Wise, for if God knew their minds were faithful he would reveal it.",
  "arab.turba.30":
    "Bacsen says that Diomedes has spoken well but has not shown how to prepare Corsufle, which the envious have confused under every kind of name. Diomedes asks Bacsen for his own view and swears by Bacsen's father that this is the head of the work, whose true beginning comes after its completion. Bacsen says that Corsufle is a compound and must be roasted seven times, because when it is perfected it dyes the whole body. The Turba agrees.",
  "arab.turba.31":
    "Pythagoras asks what they think of Bacsen's speech, since he did not give the substance its artificial names, and they ask him to name it. He says that Corsufle being its composition, they have given it the names of every body in the world, coin, copper, tin, gold, iron and lead, until it loses that colour and becomes Ixir. The Turba says he has spoken well; he says they have too, and others may speak of the remaining matters.",
  "arab.turba.32":
    "Bonellus says to Pythagoras that, as he holds, all things die and live by God's will. When the moisture is taken out of the nature and it is left for nights, it looks dead; turned and left again, as a man in his tomb, it becomes a powder. Then God gives back its soul and spirit, and the matter becomes strong, better after its corruption, as a man is stronger and younger after the resurrection. So burn the matter boldly until it is ash, for the ash receives the spirit and is soaked until it takes a finer colour than before. Painters and physicians too must first turn their colours and medicines to powder. Copper, like man, has a body and a soul: as men's breath comes from the air, copper is breathed into by the moisture from which it takes strength and multiplies, so copper burned again and again becomes better than before, for God brings many things out of one. First cooked, our copper becomes water; cooked more, it thickens into what the envious call a stone, but which is really 'an egg tending to become a metal'; broken, soaked and roasted in a stronger fire, it is coloured like blood, and laid on coins it turns them into gold, as God pleases. As sperm is made from blood only by cooking in the liver, so unless the work is cooked to powder and putrefied into a 'spiritual sperm', the colour will not come. Whoever completes it will be a prince among the people of his time.",
  "arab.turba.33":
    "Nicarus says that they have now made this secret public. The Turba says the Master ordered it; he says not the whole of it; they say the Master ordered them to clear away its darkness, so he should speak. He advises taking the gold one wishes to multiply and renew and dividing the water into two parts, and burning 'our copper' with one part: dissolved in that water, the copper is called the ferment of gold. Cooked, they melt like water, then set, crumble, and the red appears. Then soak them seven times with the remaining water until they have drunk it all and, dried out, turn into dry earth; put them on the fire for forty days until the whole putrefies and its colours appear.",
  "arab.turba.34":
    "Bacsen says that because of the previous speaker's words the philosophers said 'beware'. Take the royal Corsufle, red like copper, and grind it in calf's urine until its nature is changed, for its true nature is hidden in its belly. Asked what that nature is, he says it is a dyeing spirit it has from permanent water, coin-like and shining. Asked how it is drawn out, he says it is ground and water is poured on it seven times until it absorbs all the moisture and gains a strength equal to the fire's hostility; then it is called rust. Putrefy it carefully until it becomes a spiritual powder the colour of burnt blood, which the fire has driven into Nature's receptive belly and coloured indelibly. Kings have sought this and not found it, except those to whom God gave it. To finish, he says: whiten copper with white water, by which they also redden it, and add nothing foreign. The Turba says that he and Nictimerus have spoken well, and he invites another to continue.",
  "arab.turba.35":
    "Zimon asks whether Bacsen has left anything to say; the Turba asks him to speak. He says that only the sayings of the envious have misled seekers, for what they seek is sold for almost nothing. So the philosophers glorified this venom under every kind of name, a stone and not a stone, a gum of Ascotia. The spirit they seek is hidden in the body as the soul is in a man: unless they break down the body, soak and grind it until it is a fine, impalpable spirit, their labour is wasted; they must turn bodies into not-bodies and the bodiless into bodies, by fire and Ethelia, strong cooking and grinding not by hand, soaking, putrefaction and the sun. The thing is both common and cheap and more precious than all natures: the Art needs both, as Victimerus taught, who said that only subliming water and vapour would help. When the natures have become water and the body of Magnesia has melted, all has become vapour, and each vapour holds its equal, so 'our stag' cannot escape: the one holds back the other. Hidden in the body, it sickens and dies by rust and putrefaction; set free, it follows its spouse, and laid on coins it turns them into gold. So the work is nothing but vapour and water: two which become four, dryness and moisture, spirit and vapour. The Turba praises him as free from envy.",
  "arab.turba.36":
    "Afflontus the Philosopher says that unless they first sublime the substances by cooking, without grinding by hand, until the whole becomes water, they have not found the work. Copper has been called sand and stone, and its names change at every stage. The light, spiritual part rises to the top of the vessel and the thick part stays at the bottom: that is the philosophers' grinding, by cooking, not by hand. So Agadaimon says: cook the copper until it is soft and impalpable, and sublime it six or seven times until the water comes down. The body that was not water becomes water, mixed with the other water into one. Fools, hearing 'water', think of rain water; had they read their books they would know it is permanent water, which cannot become permanent without its companion. The philosophers call it Water of Gold, the Fiery, Good Venom, and the Sand of Many Names that Hermes ordered to be washed often to remove the blackness of the Sun. Unless they take this pure body, 'our copper', they will see nothing, for nothing foreign enters it. So set aside the crowd of obscure names, for the nature is one water; whoever goes wrong comes close to ruin. Keep the one nature and dismiss what is foreign.",
  "arab.turba.37":
    "Bonellus says that he will speak a little about Magnesia. Seal it in its vessel and cook it gently until it all becomes water; when the water is about to turn black, the body has melted. Cook it forty days until it drinks up the moisture of the vinegar and honey, then wash away the blackness until the stone is dry. So the envious said, 'wash the Magnesia with soft water and cook until it becomes earth', and then it is called copper. This is 'our copper', which the philosophers ordered to be washed with permanent water: 'divide the venom in two, burn the body with one part and putrefy it with the other'. The whole work is done by water. Repeating it for the Turba: cook our copper forty days with the first part of the water until it is a stone, cleanse it seven times and let it putrefy. The envious called it 'sufficiently black', 'sufficiently white' and 'sufficiently red' as its colours changed, and 'Ixir satis'; but you need only make the black copper white and then red. Melt, grind and cook the Ixir until the stone shines like marble, the great secret; wash it seven times with permanent water until its hidden nature is drawn out. As Maria says, sulphurs are held in sulphurs, and from sulphur mixed with sulphur comes a great work. There are two kinds of whitening and reddening, by rust and by cooking. Do not separate it from the waters, lest the poisons reach you and all is lost.",
  "arab.turba.38":
    "Effistus says that he bears witness to everything Bonellus has said. Asked to add something to make beginners bolder, he points to how Hermes, chief of the philosophers, mixed the natures: take the stone of gold, combine it with moisture, which is permanent water, put it in its vessel over a gentle fire until it melts; leave it until the water dries and the sand and water are joined; then make the fire stronger until it dries again and becomes earth. That is the beginning of the secret; repeat it many times until two-thirds of the water is gone and colours appear. Asked for more, he says whitening comes only by cooking, so Agadaimon rightly wrote about cooking, grinding and soaking Ethelia. Do not pour all the water on at once, lest the Ixir be drowned, but little by little, grinding and drying several times until the water is used up. When the envious say 'leave the water once poured in and it will sink to the bottom', they mean: when the moisture has dried and become powder, leave it in its glass vessel for forty days, until it passes through the colours the philosophers describe. By this cooking the bodies put on their spirits and spiritual tinctures and become warm. The Turba praises him and invites another to speak.",
  "arab.turba.39":
    "Bacsen says that nothing useful comes without a patient, careful soul and continual work. Whoever wants to learn too fast should not turn to their books, which must be read two or three times before their higher sense appears; the Master said that whoever bends over them in his leisure fears God and will reign in the Kingdom. People die for the smallest gain; what should they do for this almost impossible gift, which is beyond reason without divine inspiration? He once met a man who knew the elements as well as he did but failed through sadness, ignorance and haste. A man who sows expects fruit only in season; how can they expect this gift after one book? Truth is found only after error, and nothing is more painful than error in this Art, when one thinks one holds the whole world and finds nothing. The Philosopher's 'pound, cook, repeat, and do not grow weary' covers mixing, cooking, roasting, whitening, grinding, making rust and dyeing: many names, one procedure. Blend the venom rightly and cook it many times without tiring, until the Ixir is dressed in 'the garment of the Kingdom'; when it turns Tyrian colour, they have found what the philosophers found. Reading is dead speech and what is spoken with the lips is living speech: read often and think carefully about what they have said.",
  "arab.turba.40":
    "Jargus says that Bacsen has left part of his speech obscure, and Bacsen asks him to explain it. Jargus says the copper Bacsen spoke of is not copper, nor the common people's tin; it is 'our true work' (or body), which must be combined with the body of Magnesia, cooked and ground tirelessly until the stone is made. Then grind the stone in its vessel with water of nitre and melt it until it is destroyed. You need a water with which, the more you cook, the more you sprinkle, until the copper takes on rust, which is the foundation of their work. Cook and grind it with Egyptian vinegar.",
  "arab.turba.41":
    "Zimon says to Jargos that what he said is true, but the Turba has not yet spoken of the rotundum (the round thing). Asked for his view, he says that the rotundum turns into four elements and comes from one thing. Asked how to govern it, he says: take one part of 'our copper' and three parts of Permanent Water, mix and cook them until they thicken and become one stone. Of this the envious said: take one part of the pure body and three parts of copper of Magnesia, mix with purified vinegar mixed with 'male of earth', seal the vessel, watch it, and cook continually until it becomes earth.",
  "arab.turba.42":
    "Ascanius says that too much talk leads the subject further into error. When the philosophers' books say that Nature is only one and overcomes all things, know that they are one thing and one compound. A man's make-up is formed of soul and body, and so these must be joined: when the philosophers prepared the matters and joined spouses who loved each other, a golden water rose from them. The Turba complains that he has jumped from the first work to the second and made his words obscure. He gives the first work: stir up war between copper and quicksilver until they destroy and corrupt each other, for when copper conceives quicksilver it sets it, and when quicksilver conceives copper the copper hardens into earth; destroy the copper's body until it is powder. Join the male to the female, vapour and quicksilver, until they become Ethel; whoever turns them into spirit by Ethel and then reddens them dyes every body, for by careful cooking a pure, spiritual, sublime soul is drawn out of the body, which dyes every body. Asked what that body is, he says it is a natural sulphurous thing called by the names of all bodies.",
  "arab.turba.43":
    "Dardaris says that they have often spoken of the procedure and the conjunction, but the hidden soul can be drawn out only by Ethelia, by which bodies become not-bodies through continual cooking and the sublimation of Ethelia. Quicksilver is fiery and burns every body more than fire does, killing bodies, and whatever is mixed with it is ground down and destroyed. When the bodies have been carefully ground and raised as required, the Ethel nature is produced, with a dyeing colour that does not fly off, and it dyes the copper, which, as the Turba said, does not dye until it is dyed. The body of copper is governed by Magnesia; quicksilver is four bodies; and the matter exists only through moisture, being the water of sulphur, for sulphurs are held in sulphurs. Asked what sulphurs are, he says they are souls hidden in four bodies, which, drawn out by themselves, hold one another and are naturally joined; govern with water what is hidden in sulphur's belly and cleanse it, and nature rejoices to meet nature, and water its equal. The four bodies are not dyed but dye. The Turba asks why he does not say, like the ancients, that they dye once dyed; he says the four coins of the common people are not dyed, but they dye copper, and when that copper is dyed it dyes the people's coins.",
  "arab.turba.44":
    "Moyses tells Dardaris that the philosophers have called this one thing by many names, sometimes two and sometimes three. Asked to name them, he says: the one is the fiery thing, the two is the body made up in it, and the three is the water of sulphur, with which it is washed and governed until perfected. The Philosopher says that the quicksilver that dyes gold is quicksilver from Cambar; Dardaris objects that the Philosopher says sometimes from Cambar and sometimes from orpiment. Moyses answers that quicksilver of orpiment is Cambar of Magnesia, but quicksilver is the sulphur rising from the mixed compound. Mix that thick thing with fiery venom, putrefy it and grind it carefully until a spirit is produced, hidden in that other spirit, and then the tincture they all want is made.",
  "arab.turba.45":
    "Plato says that when the bodies are being dissolved they must not burn them, and must wash them with sea water until their salt turns sweet, clears, dyes, becomes the tincture of copper and flies off; for one becomes the dyer and the other the dyed, and the spirit, hidden in the other spirit, makes both volatile. So the Wise said that the gate of flight must not be opened, since flight causes death. The philosophers brought the volatile back to a body like the fixed ones from which they came and put them where they could not escape. When the Philosopher says the dyer and the dyed become one tincture, he means a spirit hidden in another moist spirit, one cold and one hot, made one though they do not suit each other. So the work is governed by bodies and not-bodies, until the bodiless become bodies that stand the fire. Join those that do not fly off; wash the body with the bodiless until the bodiless takes a body; 'convert the earth into water, water into fire, fire into air', hide the fire in the depths of the water and the earth in the belly of the air, mixing hot with moist and cold with dry. Nature overcomes Nature, Nature rejoices in Nature, Nature contains Nature.",
  "arab.turba.46":
    "Attamus says that the philosophers have often written about Rubigo (rust), but Rubigo is an invented name, not a true one. Asked for its true name, he says Rubigo belongs to the work because it comes from gold alone. Asked why the philosophers compared it to the leech, he says: because water is hidden in sulphurous gold as the leech is in water. Rubigo is the reddening of the second work, but making rubigo is whitening in the first work, in which the philosophers ordered the flower of gold to be taken with an equal share of gold.",
  "arab.turba.47":
    "Mundus says that Attamus has said enough about Rubigo, and he will speak of venom. Venom is not a body, because subtle spirits have made it a fine spirit, dyed the body and burned it with venom, which, the Philosopher says, will dye every body. The ancient philosophers thought that whoever turned gold into venom had reached the goal, and whoever cannot do so gains nothing. Unless they reduce the thing by fire until it rises like a spirit, they achieve nothing. It is a spirit that avoids the fire, a heavy smoke, which, entering the body, goes right through it and makes it rejoice. All the philosophers said: take a black, joining spirit, and with it break up the bodies and torment them until they are changed.",
  "arab.turba.48":
    "Pythagoras says that the philosophers have described the conjunction in different ways, but he tells them to make quicksilver hold the body of Magnesia, or the body Kuhul, or the Spume of the Moon, or incombustible sulphur, roasted calx, or 'alum from apples'. If any of these had a procedure of its own, a philosopher would not say so: sulphur, calx, alum from apples and Kuhul are all nothing but water of sulphur. Magnesia mixed with quicksilver and sulphur makes them pursue each other, so do not leave Magnesia without quicksilver; so compounded it is called a very strong composition, one of the ten procedures the philosophers set down. When Magnesia is whitened with quicksilver, set white water in it, and when reddened, red water, for the procedure is not one: the first setting is of tin, copper and lead, the second is made with water of sulphur. Some readers think the composition can be bought; but nothing of the work can be bought, and the whole science is vapour and the sublimation of water, with the joining of quicksilver to the body of Magnesia. The philosophers have shown that the impure water of sulphur comes from sulphur alone, and no sulphur is made without the water of its calx, of quicksilver and of sulphur.",
  "arab.turba.49":
    "Belus says that composition, contact and setting are one thing. Take a part of the one composition and a part of the ferment of gold and put pure water of sulphur on them: this is the potent (or revealed) secret that dyes every body. Pythagoras asks why he calls it that without showing the work; Belus says they found in their books what Pythagoras received from the ancients, and Pythagoras says that is why he gathered them, to clear up obscurities in books. Belus says the pure water from sulphur is not made of sulphur alone but of several things, the one sulphur being made from several sulphurs, and asks how to make them one. Pythagoras tells him to mix what fights the fire with what does not, for things joined in a suitable fire contend, just as the physician's warm poisons are cooked in a gentle fire that does not burn; the philosophers say a little sulphur burns many strong things, and the moisture left is called moist pitch, balsam of gum and so on, so the philosophers are like physicians, though the physicians' tests are harsher. Asked by the Turba to explain the procedure, Belus says the secret comes from two compositions, sulphur and magnesia. Reduced and joined into one, it is called water, spume of Boletus (a kind of fungus) and thickness of gold; reduced to quicksilver, sulphur of water; and sulphur holding sulphur is called a fiery venom, because it is an open secret that rises from the things they know.",
  "arab.turba.50":
    "Pandolphus asks Belus to describe the sublimation of sulphur; the Turba asks Pandolphus to do it. He says the philosophers ordered quicksilver to be taken from Cambar (cinnabar), which is true but a little ambiguous. Sublime the quicksilver 'in tabernacles' and draw it from Cambar; but there is another Cambar in sulphur, which Belus showed them, for many works come from sulphur mixed with sulphur. When it is sublimed, from the Cambar comes the quicksilver called Ethelia, Orpiment, Zendrio or Sanderich, Ebsemich, Magnesia, Kuhul or Chuhul, and many other names. The philosophers say that when it is governed by its procedure ('for ten is the perfection of all things'), its white nature appears without any shadow; the envious call it lead from Ebmich, Magnesia, Marteck, White Copper. Truly whitened, it has no shadow or blackness, has left its thick heavy bodies, and a clean moist spirit has risen with it, which is tincture. So the wise say that copper has a soul and a body: its soul is spirit and its body is thick. Destroy the thick body until you draw out a dyeing spirit, and mix that spirit with light sulphur until you achieve your aim.",
  "arab.turba.51":
    "Horfolcos tells Pandolphus that he has described only the last stage of the procedure, which should be explained from the beginning. First burn the copper in a gentle fire, like the heat used to hatch eggs, with its own moisture so its spirit is not burnt, in a vessel closed on all sides, so that its colour (Waite suggests 'heat') increases, the body of copper is destroyed and its dyeing spirit drawn out. The envious call it quicksilver from the Flower of Copper, the water of our copper, a fiery venom, and Ethelia drawn from many things; and they say that when all things become one, bodies become not-bodies and not-bodies bodies. Every body is dissolved by the spirit mixed with it, and every dyeing spirit that stands the fire is coloured by bodies. Blessed be the one who inspired the Wise to turn a body into a spirit, unchangeable and incorruptible, so that sulphur that flew off now stays and does not burn. Whoever can redden the fleeing spirit with its body, and then draw out the fine nature hidden in its belly, dyes every body, if he is patient. So the envious say that from copper moistened with its own moisture, ground in its water and cooked in sulphur, a body with Ethelia is drawn that is fit to dye anything. Whatever words are found in any book mean quicksilver, which they call water of sulphur, and sometimes lead, copper and 'copulated coin'.",
  "arab.turba.52":
    "Ixumdrus tells Horfolcus that he has treated the governing of copper excellently; Horfolcus asks him to complete what he left out. Ixumdrus says that the Ethelia, which the envious call by many names, whitens and, once whitened, dyes; the philosophers call it the Flower of Gold. Copper does not dye until it is dyed. Mixed with the ten 'fermented urines', all this is called Multiplication; some call their mixed bodies Corsufle and Gum of Gold. The names in the philosophers' books are true and yet invented, because they are one thing, one opinion and one way: the quicksilver drawn from all things, the pure water that destroys the shadow of copper. Whitened, it becomes a sulphur that holds sulphur, a venom shining like marble, which the envious call Ethelia, orpiment and sandarac. It is the great secret, because sulphur alone whitens copper, but only once it has itself been whitened. When this sulphur flees as vapour, hold it back with quicksilver of its own kind, for sulphurs are held by sulphurs. Do not mistake it for common coin: what the philosophers seek is the philosophers' coin. Mix quicksilver with quicksilver of its kind until they become one clean water made of two: the great secret, prepared with its own gum, cooked gently with flowers and earth, reddened with mucra, vinegar, salt and nitre, and turned into rust with 'mutal' or another of the dyeing agents.",
  "arab.turba.53":
    "Exumenus says that the envious have ruined the whole Art with their many names, but the whole work is the Art of the Coin, making coin-like gold. Asked for names, he lists salting, subliming, washing and grinding Ethelias, whitening in the fire, cooking vapour and setting, turning into rust, preparing Ethel, and the art of the water of sulphur and coagula: all name the one operation that has ground and whitened copper. Quicksilver looks white, but when the smoke of sulphur takes hold of it it reddens and becomes Cambar, so the Philosopher says the nature of lead changes quickly. They grind and repeat many times to draw out the spirits, which the fire would burn; but the water put with them shelters them in its belly, and the more the flame takes hold, the deeper they hide. The Turba says that unless bodies are made not-bodies nothing is achieved, and unless the thing is ground in the fire the Ethelia does not rise; of it Hermes says 'Sift the things you know', and another 'Melt the things'. Exumenus explains the Master's saying: a great south wind, when it rises, lifts the clouds and raises the vapours of the sea. When the Turba calls this obscure, he speaks of the vessel holding incombustible sulphur, and tells them to set flowing quicksilver out of many things, 'that two may be made three, and four one, and two one'.",
  "arab.turba.54":
    "Anaxagoras tells them to take the burnt, volatile thing that lacks a body and give it one, then take the heavy thing that has smoke and thirsts to drink. The Turba asks him to explain this obscurity without envy. He says the burnt volatile thing and the thirsty thing are Ethelia joined with sulphur: put them in a glass vessel over the fire and cook until all becomes Cambar, and God will complete the secret they seek. Cook continually and do not tire of repeating. The work is perfected by preparing water of sulphur with 'tabula' and cooking until it becomes Rubigo (rust), for all the philosophers say that whoever can turn Rubigo into golden venom has done the work, and otherwise his labour is in vain.",
  "arab.turba.55":
    "Zenon says that Pythagoras dealt with the water, which the envious call by every name, and at the end of his book with the ferment of gold, on which clean water of sulphur and a little of its gum are to be put; he is astonished that the envious speak of the completion of this work rather than its beginning. The Turba asks why he has left it to putrefy. He says that putrefaction does not happen without the dry and the moist together, but common people putrefy with the moist alone, so the moist is only set with the dry; from both together the work begins. Yet the envious have divided the work into three parts, saying that one part flees quickly and the other is fixed and immovable.",
  "arab.turba.56":
    "Constans asks what they have to do with the treatises of the envious, since the work must deal with four things. Asked which, he says: earth, water, air and fire, the four elements, without which nothing is ever born or completed in the Art. Mix the dry with the moist, earth with water, and cook in the fire and the air, by which spirit and soul are dried. The fine dyeing agent takes its strength from the fine part of the earth, the fire and the air, while from the fine part of the water a fine spirit has been dried. So the process is that everything is turned into earth once the fine parts are drawn out, because then a body is made that is a kind of airy thing, which afterwards dyes the body of coins laid on it. Do not multiply things: the envious multiplied them to ruin you, described various procedures to deceive, and called it the moist with all the moist and the dry with all the dry, by the name of every stone and metal, the gall of sea creatures, the birds of heaven and the reptiles of the earth. Bodies are dyed with bodies. As the Philosopher said briefly at the beginning of his book: in the art of gold is the quicksilver from Cambar, and in coins the quicksilver from the Male. Look no further, since the two quicksilvers are one.",
  "arab.turba.57":
    "Acratus says that he brings philosophy near to the Sun and Moon: whoever wants the truth should take the moisture of the Sun and the Spume of the Moon. The Turba asks why he opposes his brothers. He gives the procedure: take part of the coins the philosophers ordered, which Hermes adapted to true dyeing, and part of the philosophers' copper; put all four bodies in a sealed vessel and cook seven days, and the copper, ground with the coins, turns to water. Cook again; a blackness appears on top; repeat until the blackness of Kuhul is used up and a precious whiteness appears; cook until all is dry and turned to stone. Cook that stone, born of copper and coins, in a sharper fire until it is a precious ash; mix the ash with water, cook and soak it with permanent water until the compound is sweet, mild and red; cook in a hotter fire with the vessel sealed, for so fleeing bodies stop fleeing, spirits become bodies and bodies spirits, with a dyeing, sprouting soul. The Turba sums up his teaching and asks why he did not call things by their proper names. He says he has spoken as they did; Bonellus agrees, for otherwise they would not have his sayings written in their books.",
  "arab.turba.58":
    "Balgus tells Acratus that the whole Turba has spoken, but a benefactor sometimes deceives even when meaning well. He says the envious have hidden this secret in physics, astronomy and the art of images, in trees, and under the names of metals, vapours and reptiles. Beat iron into plates, sprinkle it with venom and seal it in its vessel, kneaded like a dough, neither too wet nor too dry; heap coals on it, and after some days the plates will have melted and globules will cling to the lid, for in the fire the vinegar rises; keep that part separately. By repeated cooking and grinding it sets and its nature changes. Nothing is more precious than the red sand of the sea, where the Sputum of the Moon is joined with the Sun's rays; then 'the dew of the death-dealer' is joined, and the longer it goes the harder it sets, overcoming earthly fire. Bonites asks why he has not described the tree whose fruit stops all hunger. Balgus says a man of science told him of this tree, of pure whiteness, its perfection being its fruit: build a round, dark house all round the tree, encircled by dew, put in it a man of a hundred years, and shut the door so no dust or wind gets in; after 180 days the old man, eating the fruit, becomes young. Balgus marvels at natures that turn the old man's soul into a young body and make the father into the son, and blesses God.",
  "arab.turba.59":
    "Theophilus says that he will add to what Bonites said. The philosophers told the truth when they spoke of 'water of life', because whatever is mixed with this water first dies, then lives and becomes young. Iron rusts only through this water, put in the sun until it melts, is soaked and sets; but silence is better than saying more. Pressed by the Turba, he speaks of fruits of the perfect tree that blossom at the start of summer and ripen and become sweet. So too the woman who flees from her own children, angry and unwilling to let her husband have her beauty; he loves her furiously and struggles with her until he lies with her and God perfects the foetus; but a man who comes to his wife only for lust has his beauty burnt up by fire. The dragon never dies, but the philosophers have put to death 'the woman who slays her spouses', whose belly is full of weapons and venom. Dig a grave for the dragon and bury the woman with him: the more tightly he clasps her, the more his body is cut to pieces by the weapons formed in her body; mixed with her limbs he becomes safe from death, and the whole turns to blood. The philosophers leave him in the sun until the blood dries, and find the venom now revealed; then the wind is hidden.",
  "arab.turba.60":
    "Bonellus says that nothing among the chosen things is useful without conjunction and governing, because sperm is made of blood and desire. When man and woman mingle, the sperm is fed by the moisture of the womb, the moistening blood and heat, and after forty nights it is formed; if the moisture of the blood and womb were not warm, the sperm would not dissolve and no foetus would be conceived. God has given heat and blood to feed the sperm until the child is born; after that it is fed only by milk and fire, sparingly and gradually, while it is 'dust', and the more it burns, the more its bones strengthen and it grows towards youth, when it stands on its own. They must do the same in the Art: nothing is ever born without heat. A bath that is too hot destroys the matter; one too cold drives it off and scatters it; but a tempered bath suits the body and is pleasant, smoothing the veins and adding flesh. Understand this, and fear God in all they try to govern.",
  "arab.turba.61":
    "Moses says that the envious have called 'lead of copper' the instruments of formation, to deceive later readers; but there are no instruments except their own white, strong, shining powder, and their hollow stone and marble, and no powder suits the whole work better or joins better with their compound than the powder of Alociae, from which the instruments of formation are made. The philosophers say 'take instruments from the egg', but not what the egg is, or from which bird. Governing these things is harder than the whole work, because if the compound is governed more than it should be, its light is taken away and put out by the sea; so the philosophers ordered it to be governed with deep judgement. When the moon is full, take it and put it in sand until it dissolves, with patience, or you will spoil the work; cook it in a gentle fire until it dissolves, then quench it with vinegar, and you will find one thing separated from three companions: the first, Ixir, mingles, the second burns, the third melts. First add nine ounces of vinegar twice, once while the vessel is heating and again when it is hot.",
  "arab.turba.62":
    "Mundus says that whatever the philosophers name, Kenckel, herbs, 'geldum' and 'carmen', is one thing: do not worry about many things, for there is one Tyrian tincture of the philosophers, which they have named as they liked, and, setting aside its proper name, called it black, because it was drawn from 'our sea'. The ancient priests would not wear artificial garments, and to purify the altars and bring nothing unclean into them they dyed Kenckel a Tyrian colour; but the Tyrian colour the philosophers placed in their altars and treasuries was cleaner and sweeter-smelling than he can describe, drawn from their red and purest sea, sweet-smelling and not foul even in putrefaction. All their many names for it are true. Corn being ground is an example: after grinding it has another name, and after sifting each separated part has its own name, yet at bottom there is one name, corn. So they call the purple at each stage by the name of its colour.",
  "arab.turba.63":
    "Philosophus (the Philosopher; the text gives no other name) says that the nature is male and female, which is why the envious call it the body of Magnesia, for the greatest secret is in it. Put Magnesia in its vessel and cook carefully; open it after some days and the whole will have turned to water; cook further until it sets and holds itself together. When the envious write of the sea they mean moisture, by the basket they mean the vessel, and by the medicines Nature, because it sprouts and flowers. When they say 'wash until the blackness of the copper passes away', some call this blackness coins. But Agadimon showed it plainly: when the things (or the copper) are first mixed and cooked once, the prescribed blackness appears, and they all become black. This is the lead of the Wise, of which their books speak so often; some also call it the lead of 'our black coins'.",
  "arab.turba.64":
    "Pythagoras says that it is wonderful how the philosophers differ in what they said before, and how they agree, about this small and most common thing in which the precious thing is hidden. If ordinary people knew this small, worthless thing, they would think it a lie; if they knew its power they would not despise it; but God has hidden it from the crowd, so that the world should not be laid waste.",
  "arab.turba.65":
    "Horfolcus says that Mundus has taught the Art with the clearest arguments, and whoever does not understand him is a brute beast; but he will explain the procedure so that a beginner may combine the common with the dear and the dear with the common. At the start, mix the elements raw and pure over a gentle fire, and do not raise the fire until they are joined, so that they embrace one another and are gradually burnt until dried. One spirit burns one thing, and one body strengthens one spirit to fight the fire. After the first burning, wash and whiten it on the fire until all is one colour, then mix in the rest of the moisture, and its colour will rise. Well cooked, the elements rejoice and change their natures: the molten (lead) stops melting, the moist becomes dry, the thick body becomes spirit, and the fleeing spirit becomes strong enough to fight the fire. So the Philosopher says: 'Convert the elements and you will find what you seek', that is, make the moist dry and the fleeing fixed. The death and life of the elements come from fire, and the compound sprouts by itself; when the colours begin you will see the wonders of God's wisdom, until the Tyrian colour is complete. He praises the heavenly Nature that dyes other natures and converts the elements, and makes the compound fixed and scarlet.",
  "arab.turba.66":
    "Exemiganus tells Lucas that he has rightly treated the living, hidden silver, which is Magnesia, and told later readers to test things and read the books, knowing the philosophers' saying: 'Search out the hidden spirit and do not despise it', for when it stays it is a great secret and does much good.",
  "arab.turba.67":
    "Lucas says that what he sets out is clearer than their words: the Philosopher says, 'Burn the copper, burn the silver, burn the gold.' Hermiganus says that this is darker than ever; the Turba asks him to make it clear. He says that in 'burn, burn, burn' only the names differ, for they are one and the same thing. They reproach him for being so brief and jealous, and he speaks more plainly: to whiten is to burn, and to redden is life. The envious multiplied names to mislead, but the Art is defined as the melting of the body and the separation of the soul from it, since copper, like a man, has a soul and a body. So destroy the body and draw out the soul, for, as the philosophers say, body does not penetrate body, but a subtle nature, the soul, dyes and penetrates the body. When the Turba says he is still obscure, he says the envious have said that Saturn's brightness does not appear unless it is dark when it rises in the air, that Mercury is hidden by the Sun's rays, that quicksilver brings the body to life by its fiery strength, and so the work is done; but Venus, when she rises in the east, goes before the Sun.",
  "arab.turba.68":
    "Attamus says that their work is produced by 'the generation of the sea', by which and with which, after God, it is completed. Take Halsut and old sea stones and boil them with coals until white; quench them in white vinegar: if 24 ounces have been boiled, quench the heat with a third part of the vinegar, 8 ounces; grind with white vinegar and cook in the sun and black earth for 42 days. The second work runs from the tenth day of September to the tenth day (or degree) of Libra; do not add vinegar a second time, but cook until all its vinegar has dried and it becomes a fixed earth, like Egyptian earth. One batch sets faster than another because of the cooking: in a damp, dewy place it sets more quickly, in a dry place more slowly.",
  "arab.turba.69":
    "Florus tells Mundus that he means to complete his teaching, since Mundus has not finished explaining the cooking; Mundus tells him to go on. Florus says that the sign of a good first cooking is that its redness is drawn out. Asked what redness is, he says: when the matter is entirely black, whiteness is hidden in the belly of that blackness, and it must be drawn out very subtly. In the second cooking, put that whiteness in a vessel with its instruments and cook gently until it is completely white; when whiteness appears and spreads over everything, redness is hidden in it, but do not draw it out: cook until the whole is the deepest red. The first blackness comes from the nature of Marteck, and the redness drawn from it has improved the black and made peace between the fleeing and the non-fleeing, making the two one. Asked why, he says that the tormented matter, sunk in the body, changes it into a nature that cannot be altered or erased. Know the sulphur that blackens the body: it cannot be handled, but torments and dyes; it does not open the door to the fugitive, and turns fugitive with the fugitive. Its tormenting is not harmful or corrupting but unites things usefully, otherwise it would not be embraced until its fast colours were drawn out. This is what they call water of sulphur, prepared for the red tinctures; the blackening, without which nothing comes to pass, he declares to be the key of the work.",
  "arab.turba.70":
    "Mundus says that 'the head is all things', and without it all that is added is useless. So the Masters said that what is perfected is one, and many natures do not improve it, but one suitable nature, governed carefully, for some have gone wrong through ignorance of governing. Do not heed the many compositions the philosophers list in their books, for the nature of truth is one, and the followers of Nature call it the one thing in whose belly the natural secret is hidden, seen and known only by the Wise. Whoever can draw out its make-up and govern it evenly will see a nature rise from it that conquers all natures, and the Masters' saying will be fulfilled: 'Nature rejoices in Nature, Nature overcomes Nature, and Nature contains Nature'; yet there are not many natures, but one that has its own natures and properties, by which it prevails. The Master began with one and ended with one, and so called those unities Sulphureous Water, which conquers all Nature.",
  "arab.turba.71":
    "Bracus says that Mundus has described this sulphurous water elegantly. Unless solid bodies are destroyed by a nature that has no body, until they become not-bodies and like the finest spirit, you cannot reach the fine, dyeing soul hidden in the natural belly; and unless the body is withered and destroyed until it dies, and its soul, a dyeing spirit, is drawn out of it, you cannot dye a body with it.",
  "arab.turba.72":
    "Philosophus (the Philosopher) says that the first composition, the body of Magnesia, is made of several things that become one, which the ancients called Albar of copper; governed, it has ten names, from the colours that appear in it, but all mean Albar of copper, because it dyes every body that enters the composition. Composition is moist and dry; cooked wisely they become one, 'the good thing of several names'. Red, it is called Flower of Gold, Ferment of Gold, Gold of Coral, red sulphur and red orpiment. He turns to the fire and the days, so that whoever owns this book may be free from poverty: there are many kinds of fire, of straw and ash, of coals, with flame and without; and lead is lead of copper, which holds the whole secret. When the elements are turned into lead of copper they become one new nature, put in a glass vessel, and take on a venerable redness. Though the philosophers often say 'take gold', it need be done only once. Democritus's lists, from bottom to top and top to bottom, of iron, lead, copper, coin and the golds of coral and crocus show that only half-gold is taken. Gold is not turned to rust without lead and copper and the vinegar the wise know; 'gold of coral' and 'purple gold' are only names of colours that come from the vinegar. Each colour must be cooked forty days until dry, then soaked and cooked; its grades are citrine, then red, then like dry crocus; and so it is laid on coin.",
  "arab.turba.conclusion":
    "Agmon says that he adds this as a corollary. Whoever does not melt and set goes badly wrong. Make the earth black, separate its soul and its water, then whiten it, and you will find what you seek. Whoever makes earth black, dissolves it with fire until it is like a naked sword, and fixes the whole with consuming fire deserves to be called happy and will be raised above the circle of the world. That is enough about the revealing of their stone for the Sons of the Doctrine. Its strength never decays, but grows when it is put in the fire; dissolve it and it dissolves, set it and it sets. No one is without it, yet everyone needs it. It has many names, yet is called by one only, and can be hidden if need be. It is 'a stone and not a stone, spirit, soul, and body'; white, volatile, hollow, hairless and cold, yet no one can put his tongue to it unharmed. If you want it to fly, it flies; call it water and you speak truly, say it is not water and you speak falsely. Do not be misled by its many names: it is one thing, to which nothing foreign is added. Look for its place and add nothing foreign. If the names had not been multiplied to deceive ordinary people, many would mock their wisdom.",
};
